    "line3": "",
    "lead": "It picks up the commands from your radio and passes them to the flight controller. All four versions work with a radio that has 2.4 GHz ExpressLRS. Not sure which to pick? Take Lite for a 3-inch build or Lite-UFL for a 5-inch. Take Mono or Gemini only if the radio also has a sub-GHz ExpressLRS module."
  },
  "whatIsThis": {
    "intro": "A receiver has one job: get your commands off the air and hand them to the flight controller. OpenRX runs ExpressLRS, which uses LoRa modulation: the signal is a frequency chirp. That is why a receiver this small keeps a robust link on the same 2.4 GHz band where Wi-Fi drops out. It speaks CRSF to the flight controller over one UART wire, is powered from a 5V pad, and flashes over Wi-Fi or Betaflight passthrough. Four variants, from ceramic antenna Lite to dual-radio Gemini.",
    "needs": [
      "A radio with an ExpressLRS transmitter, internal or module, on 2.4 GHz",
      "The same binding phrase flashed on both ends",
      "A flight controller with a free UART and a 5 V pad",
      "The rest of the aircraft: it only carries commands, it moves nothing"
    ],
    "fit": "The aircraft's ear, the first stop for everything you do on the sticks.",
    "chain": "rx"
  },
